 ### [Density Range Windowing](1571-density-range-windowing.md)
-Density Range Windowing restricts rendering to voxels whose density falls within a specified $[min, max]$ range, allowing selective visualization of specific structures within a volume. It sets itself apart from its parent method, Grid Visualization with Thresholding, by operating on a continuous density interval rather than a single binary opacity threshold or gradient magnitude, enabling finer control over semi-transparent vs. dense core separation.
+Density Range Windowing restricts rendering to voxels whose density falls within a specified $[min, max]$ range, allowing selective visualization of specific structures within a volume. It sets itself apart from its parent method, Grid Visualization with Thresholding, by operating on a continuous density interval rather than a single binary opacity threshold or gradient magnitude, enabling finer control over semi-transparent vs. dense core separation. It is chosen when volumetric opacity integration is required rather than geometric abstraction.
 
 ### [Glyph Lines](8934-glyph-lines.md)
-Glyph Lines is a sparse visualization technique for 3D vector fields that samples the field on a 2D grid at a fixed coordinate and renders each sample as a line segment. It solves the problem of visualizing flow direction and magnitude in a specific slice of a volumetric dataset without the computational cost of full volume ray casting. It differs from its parent method, Grid Visualization with Thresholding, by explicitly encoding vector direction and magnitude into geometric primitives (lines) rather than plotting scalar grid cells as points.
+Glyph Lines is a sparse visualization technique for 3D vector fields that samples the field on a 2D grid at a fixed coordinate and renders each sample as a line segment. It solves the problem of visualizing flow direction and magnitude in a specific slice of a volumetric dataset without the computational cost of full volume ray casting. It differs from its parent method, Grid Visualization with Thresholding, by explicitly encoding vector direction and magnitude into geometric primitives (lines) rather than plotting scalar grid cells as points. It is chosen when vector field topology and flow visualization are required.
 
